---
<QuestionLayout
  title="Can I share business logic between an iOS app and an Android app with Traverse?"
  description="Not through a shipped SDK on both platforms today. iOS now has a production wasmi-based runtime bridge; Android has a working, unreleased embedder. The contract you write now carries over once both are equally shipped."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/can-i-share-logic-between-ios-and-android-apps.html"
  category="Question"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>This is exactly the problem Traverse is built to eventually solve for mobile, and it's worth being precise about the word "eventually," because it isn't there yet for both platforms simultaneously.</p>

  <p>On iOS, <code>packages/swift/TraverseEmbedder</code> now ships a production runtime bridge: <code>WasmiHostBridgeClient</code> embeds the <code>wasmi</code> interpreter behind a narrow C ABI, packaged as the <code>TraverseSwiftHost</code> XCFramework, with host-enforced memory and fuel limits. That's the certification path defined by Spec 074 and governed by ADR-0014 and ADR-0015 — it replaced the earlier WasmKit-based bridge, which is now deprecated and kept only for source compatibility.</p>

  <p>On Android, <code>packages/kotlin/TraverseEmbedder</code> has a working runtime bridge built on Chicory, with bundle validation and lifecycle handling done. What's left is Compose reference-app integration and a public release — real, tracked, in-progress work, not a stalled idea.</p>

  <h2>What you can do about it now</h2>
  <p>Write the shared rule as a well-scoped Traverse capability today, targeting the platforms with the most mature support — native desktop, the browser, and now iOS. The contract doesn't change when the Android embedder ships too; the work isn't wasted, it's just not deployable to both phones on equal footing yet. Watch <a href="/platforms.html">Platforms</a> for exactly when that changes, rather than guessing from a roadmap date.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
